[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Router6’s host firewall (iptables), specifically the IPv4 filter FORWARD chain. Mechanism: all transit traffic arriving on router6’s uplink (eth2, 10.50.1.2/24 toward router3) is being dropped by explicit DROP rules, so traffic from the rest of the network cannot be forwarded into Location C LANs (172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24). This makes Location C appear “gone” from Location A.

Localization and evidence:
- From PC4/PC5 (172.16.10.0/24), pings to 172.18.51.x time out, but they can reach other remote subnets (e.g., 192.168.200.13), proving general WAN routing is fine.
- Router0/Router3 routing tables contain valid paths to 172.18.51.0/24 via router3→10.50.1.1→router6, confirming control-plane reachability.
- Router3 can ping router6’s 172.18.51.254 (eth1), but cannot ping a LAN host 172.18.51.15; meanwhile router6 itself can ping 172.18.51.15, pinpointing the block at router6’s forwarding plane.
- On router6, iptables shows:
  - “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP”
  - “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP”
  - “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP”
  Only a narrow exception exists for TCP/80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1/32. IP forwarding is enabled (net.ipv4.ip_forward=1), so the firewall is the blocker.

Root cause: Misconfigured iptables rules on router6’s FORWARD chain that drop all traffic arriving on eth2. 

Corrective fix: Remove or adjust the DROP rules on router6 to allow forwarding from eth2 to LANs (e.g., flush/filter FORWARD chain or add ACCEPT rules for established/related and needed protocols), restoring transit to 172.18.50/24 and 172.18.51/24. Example: iptables -F FORWARD; or explicitly permit eth2→eth0/eth1 traffic with stateful ACCEPT rules. === END ===